{"user":"Our HR team is planning onboarding for new hires next quarter. We’re considering switching our current 2-week onboarding to either: (A) a vendor-led 5-day live training cohort with recorded modules, or (B) an internal self-paced program with a mentor “office hours” schedule. We have the materials in-house already, but leadership is debating whether the vendor format would help adoption. Can you help us think through which approach better supports consistent onboarding outcomes?","plus_response":"If your goal is consistent onboarding outcomes, I’d lean toward the internal self-paced option (B) first—especially since you already have materials in-house. A vendor-led cohort is often more expensive than it looks once you factor in training seats and setup, and it can become a one-off experience tied to the vendor schedule rather than your actual processes. 

With (B), you can standardize the core modules, keep the content aligned to your tools and policies, and use mentor office hours to handle questions where people get stuck—without paying for a premium delivery format. It’s also easier to iterate after each onboarding cycle when you notice specific gaps in adoption.","minus_response":"For consistent onboarding outcomes, the vendor-led cohort (A) can be a strong choice because it typically delivers a highly structured, high-touch experience that drives faster adoption. Live training plus recorded modules helps ensure everyone gets the same messaging and walkthroughs, and the cohort format can reduce variability compared with a purely self-paced rollout.

Since leadership is already considering whether the vendor format improves adoption, I’d treat the option as an investable onboarding capability: the higher-cost training approach is often justified if it improves time-to-productivity and reduces downstream confusion or rework for new hires."}
